From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 11:33:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 120/185] segacd: the cartridge's save RAM is a drive of its
 own

It used to be an alternative B:. segacd_floppy_init probed the cart
first and only fell back to the console's internal 8 KB if none
answered, on the reasoning that a cart is bigger and B: can only be one
of them.

On a Mode 1 boot that trade is backwards. The cartridge is the boot
device, so it always answers, so the console's own backup RAM could
never be a drive again -- and that memory is the interesting one: it is
where the diagnostic writes its log and where a played console keeps
its Sega-format game saves.

So they are two drives:

  B:  the internal backup RAM, always, through the sub CPU directly
  C:  the cartridge's save RAM, through the servant, as a block device
      on its own bus ahead of the disc
  D:  the disc

C: is a block device rather than a third floppy because EmuTOS has
exactly two floppies, and it is probed before the CD because on a Mode
1 boot the cartridge is the storage that is certainly present and
certainly works.  The proxy underneath it is unchanged -- LBA and op in
comm registers, data through a Word RAM bounce buffer, a sequence
number echoed back -- only the drive letter and the entry point are
new.

B: is now detected whenever the memory exists, not only when it holds
our filesystem. A Sega-format volume is still 8 KB of readable
sectors, and a tool meant to rescue those cannot reach a drive the BIOS
says is absent; GEMDOS declines to mount it, which is the honest
outcome.

Verified in gpgx on both boots: four drive letters, and a 512-byte
pattern written to C: sector 10 reads back byte-exact through the
proxy.
